This is an **allergy**, an exaggerated response of the immune system to certain antigens (allergens) present in the environment, here set off by the sudden change from the hot, dusty outdoors to a cold air-conditioned room.

Symptoms (any two):
- **sneezing**;
- **watery eyes**;
- **running nose**;
- **difficulty in breathing**.

How the body responds:
- The body produces antibodies of the **IgE** type against the allergen.
- The allergen causes **mast cells** to **release chemicals like histamine and serotonin**, which produce the symptoms above.

So the body's "combat" is this exaggerated immune response. The symptoms can be reduced by drugs such as **anti-histamines, adrenalin and steroids**.

- **Trichoderma** species are **free-living fungi** that are very common in the root ecosystems. They are effective **biocontrol agents of several plant pathogens**.
- **Baculoviruses** (especially those of the genus Nucleopolyhedrovirus) are pathogens that attack insects and other arthropods. Any two of the following make them good biocontrol agents:
  - they are **species-specific**, with **narrow-spectrum insecticidal applications**;
  - they have **no negative impact on plants, mammals, birds, fish or even non-target insects**;
  - so they are especially desirable in **integrated pest management (IPM)** programmes and when an **ecologically sensitive area** is being treated.

Organic farmers use them instead of chemical pesticides, which kill useful as well as harmful organisms and pollute the environment.

(a) The microbe is the protozoan parasite **Entamoeba histolytica**, which lives in the large intestine of humans and causes amoebiasis (amoebic dysentery).

(b) The patient must have taken **contaminated drinking water or contaminated food**. Houseflies act as mechanical carriers, carrying the parasite from the faeces of infected persons to food and food products and contaminating them.

(c) Suggestions (any two):
- drink **clean (boiled or filtered) drinking water**;
- eat **non-contaminated food**, and wash vegetables and fruits properly;
- maintain **personal hygiene**, such as washing hands before eating;
- ensure **periodic cleaning and disinfection of water reservoirs**, pools and tanks, and proper disposal of waste;
- control houseflies and keep food covered.

(a) During his extensive explorations of the wilderness of the South American jungles, Alexander von Humboldt observed that within a region, **species richness increased with increasing explored area, but only up to a limit**.

(b) The relationship between species richness and area for a wide variety of taxa turns out to be a rectangular hyperbola, \(S = CA^{Z}\), which on a logarithmic scale is a straight line, \(\log S = \log C + Z \log A\). In it:
- **S** = **species richness**;
- **A** = **area**;
- **Z** = **slope of the line** (the regression coefficient);
- **C** = **Y-intercept**.

Regardless of the taxonomic group or region, Z usually lies in the range 0.1 to 0.2; for very large areas such as whole continents it is much steeper, about 0.6 to 1.2.
